\section{What is measured, and where}{


At each scan value the model is projected with \code{\link[=projectUntilSettled]{projectUntilSettled()}}, which
stops as soon as it recognises that the model has settled onto a fixed point
or onto a limit cycle, and reports which of the two happened. What happens
next depends on that answer:

\describe{
\item{A fixed point}{The state does not change, so there is nothing to
average. The quantity is read off the settled state with no further
projection at all, and the reported minimum and maximum are equal to it.}
\item{A limit cycle}{The model is projected for \strong{exactly one period} of
the detected cycle and the quantity is averaged over it, which is its
long-term average. The minimum and maximum over the cycle are reported
too.}
\item{Neither}{The model did not settle within \code{t_max} years, or a species
went extinct. The quantity is averaged over the last \code{t_sample} years and
the scan values concerned are named in a message, because those points
should not be relied on.}
}

Averaging over exactly one period is both faster and more accurate than
averaging over a fixed number of years. A window that is not a whole number
of periods leaves a residue of the oscillation in the average, which shows up
as a jagged curve. The window is rounded to a whole number of time steps, so
it can differ from the true period by up to \code{dt/2}; if you need the average
more accurately, reduce \code{dt} rather than lengthening the window, because a
longer window that is not a whole number of periods is worse, not better.
}

\section{Writing the two functions}{


\code{set_func(params, value)} takes a \code{MizerParams} object and one entry of
\code{scan_values} and returns a modified \code{MizerParams}. It must be \strong{idempotent}
— \code{set_func(set_func(p, v), v)} must give the same thing as
\code{set_func(p, v)} — because with \code{continuation = TRUE} it is applied to the
object it returned at the previous scan value. Setting something is
idempotent; appending something is not, so a function that adds a gear must
check whether the gear is already there. See \code{\link[=scanFishingMortality]{scanFishingMortality()}} for a
worked example.

There is no \code{effort} argument, because there does not need to be one:
\code{\link[=project]{project()}} and \code{\link[=projectUntilSettled]{projectUntilSettled()}} both take the fishing effort from
\code{params@initial_effort}, so a \code{set_func()} that changes the effort is all it
takes to scan over effort, and a scan over something else never has to
mention fishing at all.

\code{value_func(sim)} takes a \code{MizerSim} and returns either a time by series
matrix, as \code{\link[=getBiomass]{getBiomass()}}, \code{\link[=getYield]{getYield()}}, \code{\link[=getSSB]{getSSB()}}, \code{\link[=getN]{getN()}} and
\code{\link[=sizeIntegral]{sizeIntegral()}} all do, or a plain numeric vector over time, as
\code{\link[=getMeanWeight]{getMeanWeight()}} does. When it returns a matrix carrying \code{value_name} and
\code{units} attributes — which all of mizer's \code{MizerSim} methods do — those are
used for the y-axis label unless you override them.

Note that at a fixed point \code{value_func()} is handed a simulation with a
single time step, so a function that needs more than one time step will not
work there. Set \code{sample_all = TRUE} to force the sampling projection at every
scan value.

Neither function can be given extra arguments through \code{...}, which is
reserved for \code{distance_func}. Use a closure instead, for example
\code{value_func = function(sim) getBiomass(sim, min_w = 10)}.
}

